+# Cover archival
+
+New archival uses uncompressed zip files. A cover ID is a ten-digit decimal
+number. The first four digits select a one-million-cover item, the next two
+select a 10,000-cover batch, and the last four identify the cover in the batch.
+For example, 8,012,345 becomes `0008012345`: item `covers_0008`, batch
+`covers_0008_01.zip`, member `0008012345.jpg`. Size variants use separate
+items and zips, such as `s_covers_0008/s_covers_0008_01.zip`, with members
+`0008012345-S.jpg` (`-M` and `-L` for medium and large). Local staging paths
+start at `data_root/items/`. Remote URLs use
+`https://archive.org/download/<item>/<zip>/<member>`.
+
+`archive.archive(test=False)` stages one batch and marks its valid covers as
+archived while retaining local images. `Batch(item_id, batch_id).process_pending`
+checks all four zip files against archive.org. Pass `upload=True` to upload
+missing files and `finalize=True` to update database filenames and uploaded
+status, then delete local images and staged zips. Verification must succeed for
+all four sizes before finalization. A per-batch file lock prevents concurrent
+staging/finalization of the same batch; retries skip existing zip members.
+
+`failed` marks covers whose local files are incomplete. These are excluded from
+finalization until repaired. Legacy tar records remain readable through the
+existing tar reader.
